            0.57,
            0.084,
            0.883,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the process of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "churning",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " curd using a big mathni (churner) to separate butter. In this process, the butter being lighter floats at the top, while the buttermilk is left behind."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.569,
            0.207,
            0.883,
            0.268
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Their stay at Maasi’s place has become enjoyable and they are looking forward to"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.268,
            0.883,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "share all their memories with their friends upon returning home. Now, it is time for them to reach their final destination of the trip, Shillong, the capital of Meghalaya."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.329,
            0.883,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "On their arrival at their Bua’s (paternal aunt’s) house in Shillong, they notice a carpenter making a wooden door. While working, he accidentally drops a few iron nails in the sawdust."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.41,
            0.823,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.313,
                0.41,
                0.823,
                0.698
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.446,
                0.706,
                0.692,
                0.723
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.12: Magnetic separation"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.74,
            0.883,
            0.864
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The carpenter starts handpicking the iron nails. The children tell the carpenter to wait. They get a magnet from their Bua. They ask the carpenter to move the magnet through the sawdust. All the nails get attracted to it (Fig. 9.12). Which method of separation did the carpenter use? Recall the chapter ‘Exploring Magnets’."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.864,
            0.883,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The substances which are attracted towards a magnet are called magnetic substances. Iron is a common example"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
